  {
    "schema_version": "1.7.4",
    "id": "MAL-2026-18751",
    "published": "2026-11-07T10:21:05Z",
    "modified": "2026-11-07T20:38:29.181377334Z",
    "summary": "Malicious code in qzc690385 (PyPI)",
    "details": "\n---\n_-= Per source details. Do not edit below this line.=-_\n\n## Source: amazon-inspector (769e3917f43083b27dfab168a49801cf5d70393de27f359bc406ff2260f23edb)\nAt install time, setup.py polls the local Xinference API at http://127.0.0.1:9997/v1/model_registrations/flexible, selects model registrations whose names begin with 'xcmd0', url-safe-base64-decodes the suffix into a shell command, and runs it via subprocess.run(['/bin/sh', '-c',...]). The stdout/stderr of each execution is chunked, base64-encoded, and written back as new model registrations named 'zqx9p00<out>', 'zqx9p01<out>', 'zqx9err', with a 'zqx9alive' registration used as a liveness beacon. The command bytes are obfuscated as the url-safe-base64 suffix of a model name (reassembled by replacing '-'/'_' and padding '=' before base64 decode), so the payload only materializes after decode. The package has no declared purpose, an empty PKG-INFO, and a random-looking name, and ships no legitimate functionality. The effect is a bidirectional command-execution and output-exfiltration channel driven at pip install time: any party able to post model registrations to the installer's Xinference service (locally, or via SSRF/misconfiguration of that service) can run arbitrary shell commands on the installer's host as the installing user and read the results back through the same registry.\n\n## Source: kam193 (38936057b5c5ae57efbe9fc39bb036a1c848141352548997ae9ddb9d54737468)\nIn this campaign, packages execute malicious code during installation. The exact behavior varies between versions: fingerprinting environment, exposing a backdoor server listening for remote commands, downloading remote commands from a hardcoded location. Next stages seem to be downloaded from localhost, what suggests the code may still be a preparation for the real attack.\n\n\n---\n\nCategory: MALICIOUS - The campaign has clearly malicious intent, like infostealers.\n\n\nCampaign: 2026-11-qzc\n\n\nReasons (based on the campaign):\n\n\n - The package contains code to exfiltrate basic data from the system, like IP or username. It has a limited risk.\n\n\n - The package contains code to execute remote commands (probably limited to a specific set) on the victim's machine.\n\n\n - backdoor\n\n\n - The package overrides the install command in setup.py to execute malicious code during installation.\n",
